<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-logic-truth-tables" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Truth Tables</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Translate between English sentences and symbolic notation.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use truth tables to find truth values of basic statements.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use truth tables to find truth values of complex statements.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>
<subsection>
	<title>Truth Tables</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
			In logic, we can use a <term>truth table</term><idx>truth table</idx> to analyze a complex statement by summarizing all its possibilities and their <term>truth values</term><idx>truth value</idx> (true or false). To do this, we break the statement down into its smallest elements, the propositions. We can then see the outcome of the complex statement for all possible combinations of true and false for the propositions.
		</p>
		<p>
			For example, suppose we have the following two propositions:
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						Let <m>R</m> be <q>You paid your rent this month.</q>
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Let <m>E</m> be <q>You paid your electric bill this month.</q>
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</p>
		<p>
			We use these two propositions to demonstrate the truth tables for <em>not</em>, <em>and</em>, and <em>or</em>.
			Before reading on, make sure you have reviewed the logical symbols shown in the <xref text="title" ref="assemblage-logical-symbols"/> block of <xref ref="sec-logic-propositions"/>.
			<!--was 47% 47%-->
		</p>
			<sidebyside widths="60% 34%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
				<p>
					To set up a truth table, we list all the possible truth value combinations in a systematic way. One way to do this is to make the first column half true, then half false, then cut the pattern in half with each succeeding column. For two propositions, the first two columns are filled in to the right.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell> <m>R</m> </cell>
						<cell> <m>E</m> </cell>
						<cell> <m>R \land E </m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</sidebyside>
	
		<p>
			The four possible combinations are
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							Row 1: You have paid your rent and electric bill.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Row 2: You have paid your rent but not your electric bill.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Row 3: You have not paid your rent but you have paid your electric bill.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Row 4: You haven't paid either your rent or your electric bill (yet).
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
		</p>
		<p>
			Once we fill in the starting columns, we add additional columns for the more complex statements. We can add as many columns as needed. Below are the basic truth tables for <em>not</em>, <em>and</em>, and <em>or</em>.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<subsubsection>
		<title>Truth Tables for NOT, AND, and OR</title>
		<sidebyside widths="60% 34%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
			<p>
				<term>Not</term><idx><h>truth table</h><h>for negation</h></idx>: In the <m>\neg R</m> column, the truth value is the opposite of the value for <m>R</m>. For example, if <m>R</m> is true (you paid your rent), then <m>\neg R</m> (you did not pay your rent) is false.
			</p>
			<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
				<row header="yes">
					<cell right="minor"><m>R</m></cell>
					<cell right="minor"><m>\neg R</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</sidebyside>
		<sidebyside widths="60% 34%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
			<p>
				<term>And</term><idx><h>truth table</h><h>for and</h></idx>: For <m>R \land E</m> to be true, you must have paid both your rent and your electric bill. Otherwise, <m>R \land E</m> is false.
			</p>
			<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
				<row header="yes">
					<cell right="minor"><m>R</m></cell>
					<cell right="minor"><m>E</m></cell>
					<cell right="minor"><m>R \land E</m></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
					<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
					<cell right="minor">F</cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</sidebyside>
		<sidebyside widths="60% 34%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
			<p>
				<term>Or</term><idx><h>truth table</h><h>for or</h></idx>: For <m>R \lor E</m> to be true, you must have paid either your rent or your electric bill, or both (inclusive or). Otherwise, <m>R \lor E</m> is false.
			</p>
			<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell right="minor"><m>R</m></cell>
						<cell right="minor"><m>E</m></cell>
						<cell right="minor"><m>R \lor E</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
						<cell right="minor">F</cell>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell right="minor">F</cell>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
						<cell right="minor">T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell right="minor">F</cell>
						<cell right="minor">F</cell>
						<cell right="minor">F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
		</sidebyside>
	</subsubsection>
	<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-conditional-truth-table">
		<title>Truth Tables for the Conditional</title>
		<p>
			In <xref ref="sec-logic-propositions"/> we considered a friend's warning: <q>If you post that photo to social media, you'll lose your job.</q> The warning turns out to be wrong in only one situation: you post the photo and keep your job.
		</p>
		<assemblage>
			<title>Remember</title>
			<p>
				To better understand the conditional, think of it as a promise. A promise is false only if it is broken; otherwise, it is true.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
		<p>
			If you don't post the photo, the warning doesn't apply. Whether you keep your job or lose it for some other reason, your friend's prediction hasn't been broken, so we can't call it false.
		</p>
		<p>
			To build the truth table, let <m>P</m> be <q>You post the photo</q> and <m>L</m> be <q>You lose your job.</q> Each of the four rows matches one of the possible outcomes.
		</p>
		<sidebyside widths="60% 34%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
			<p>
				<term>If, then</term><idx><h>truth table</h><h>for a conditional</h></idx>: In the <m>P\rightarrow L</m> (read <q>If <m>P</m>, then <m>L</m></q>) column, the only false row is the one where you post the photo and don't lose your job. Otherwise, <m>P\rightarrow L</m> is true.
			</p>
			<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
			<row header="yes">
				<cell><m>P</m></cell>
				<cell><m>L</m></cell>
				<cell><m>P\rightarrow L</m></cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>T</cell>
				<cell>T</cell>
				<cell>T</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>T</cell>
				<cell>F</cell>
				<cell>F</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>F</cell>
				<cell>T</cell>
				<cell>T</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>F</cell>
				<cell>F</cell>
				<cell>T</cell>
			</row>
			</tabular>
		</sidebyside>
		<p>
			The last two rows may seem strange at first. When the hypothesis (the <q>if</q> part) is false, the conditional promises nothing, so there is nothing to break, and we count it as true. In short, a conditional is true unless the hypothesis is true and the conclusion is false.
		</p>
	</subsubsection>
	</subsection>
	<subsection>
		<title>Truth Tables for Complex Statements</title>
		<p>
			Truth tables really become useful when we seek to analyze complex statements composed of multiple propositions. To do so, we need truth tables with several columns. A good basic strategy for solving such problems is to work from the inside out and create a column in the table for each intermediate statement.
			Each intermediate statement (and hence column) should require only a single logical operation to compute. This can be tedious for long problems but guards against error.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Create a truth table for the statement <m>A \lor (\neg B)</m>.
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
			<sidebyside widths="47% 47%" margins="0% 0%" valign="top">
				<p>
					When we create the truth table, we start with columns for the propositions, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. Then we add a column for <m>\neg B</m> because that is part of the final statement. Our last column is the final statement <m>A \lor (\neg B)</m>.
					It is tempting to skip the column for <m>\neg B</m> and hold that intermediate value in your head as you compute <m>A \lor (\neg B)</m>. Resist this urge, as skipping intermediate steps often leads to errors in logic later in a complex problem.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>A</m></cell>
						<cell><m>B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>A \lor (\neg B)</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
		</sidebyside>
		<sidebyside widths="47% 47%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
				<p>
					To complete the third column, <m>\neg B</m>, we take the opposite of the <m>B</m> column. Then to complete the fourth column, we only look at the <m>A</m> and the <m>\neg B</m> columns and compare them using <em>or</em>.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row  header="yes">
						<cell><m>A</m></cell>
						<cell><m>B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>A \lor (\neg B)</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</sidebyside>
		</solution>
		</example>
		<example xml:id="ex-logic-xor">
			<statement>
				<p>
					In <xref ref="sec-logic-propositions"/>, an offer of soup <em>or</em> salad meant one or the other, but not both. Let <m>A</m> be <q>You get soup</q> and <m>B</m> be <q>You get salad.</q> Create a truth table for the statement <m>(A \lor B) \land \neg(A \land B)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Working from the inside out, we need columns for <m>A \lor B</m> and <m>A \land B</m>, then the negation <m>\neg(A \land B)</m>, and finally the whole statement. The last column combines the third and fifth columns using <em>and</em>.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>A</m></cell>
						<cell><m>B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>A \lor B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>A \land B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg(A \land B)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(A \lor B) \land \neg(A \land B)</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					The statement is true when exactly one of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is true: you get soup or salad, but not both. This is the <em>exclusive or</em><idx><h>exclusive or</h><h>truth table</h></idx>, often abbreviated XOR and written <m>A \oplus B</m>. Since we can build it from <em>and</em>, <em>or</em>, and <em>not</em>, we don't need a separate basic truth table for it.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection>
			<title>Truth Tables with Three Propositions</title>
		<p>
			To create a truth table with three propositions we need eight rows to list all possible truth combinations. Each new proposition doubles the number of rows, so three propositions need <m>2^3 = 8</m> rows. First, we will determine the columns we need to get to our final statement. Next, we will fill in the first three columns using the same method as before. Start with half true, half false, then cut the pattern in half each time.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Create a truth table for the statement <m>A \land \neg(B \lor C)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					First, write out the columns we need. We need columns for <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>, then for the statement in the parentheses, <m>(B \lor C)</m>. Then we need the negation of that column, <m> \neg(B \lor C)</m>. The last column we need is the final statement, <m>A \land \neg(B \lor C)</m>. 
				</p>
				<p>
					Here is the initial table:
				</p>
				<tabular halign= "center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row  header="yes">
						<cell><m>A</m></cell>
						<cell><m>B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>C</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(B \lor C)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg(B \lor C)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>A \land\neg(B \lor C)</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					Now we complete the columns one at a time. We use the <m>B</m> column and <m>C</m> column to complete <m>B \lor C</m>. Then, <m>\neg(B \lor C)</m> is the opposite of that column. For the final column we only need to look at the first and fifth columns (<m>A</m> and <m>\neg(B \lor C)</m>) and combine them with AND. Here is the completed table.
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row  header="yes">
						<cell><m>A</m></cell>
						<cell><m>B</m></cell>
						<cell><m>C</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(B \lor C)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg(B \lor C)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>A \land\neg(B \lor C)</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					For this statement, <m>A</m> must be true and neither <m>B</m> nor <m>C</m> can be true, so <m>A \land\neg(B \lor C)</m> is only true in the fourth row. 
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			Perhaps the last example seems theoretical. What kind of application might require this kind of logic?  For an application, consider the following propositions taken from professional baseball:
		</p>
		<p>
			Let <m>A</m> be <q>Anaheim wins,</q> <m>B</m> be <q>Baltimore wins,</q> and <m>C</m> be <q>Cleveland wins.</q>
			Suppose that Anaheim will make the playoffs if: (1) Anaheim wins, and (2) neither Baltimore nor Cleveland wins. When will Anaheim make the playoffs?  Only in the case that the statement <m>A \land \neg(B \lor C)</m> is true!  
			As we saw above, TFF (<m>A=</m> T, <m>B=</m> F, and <m>C=</m> F) is the only scenario in which Anaheim will make the playoffs.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Construct a truth table for the statement <m>(m \land\neg\,p)\rightarrow r</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Here are the columns for the table: 
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>m</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>r</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg\,p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>m \land\neg\,p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(m \land\neg\,p)\rightarrow r</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
						<cell></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					For the fourth column, we take the opposite of <m>p</m>. Then we use the first and fourth columns to complete <m>m \land \neg\,p</m>. Next, we use columns five and three to complete our conditional statement. 
					Be careful: the hypothesis (column five) is to the right of the conclusion (column three), so read these two columns from right to left. Here is the completed table:
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell><m>m</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>r</m></cell>
						<cell><m>\neg\,p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>m \land\neg\,p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(m \land\neg\,p)\rightarrow r</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					When <m>m</m> is true, <m>p</m> is false, and <m>r</m> is false<mdash/>the fourth row of the table<mdash/>then the hypothesis <m>m \land \neg\,p</m> will be true, but the conclusion (<m>r</m>) is false, so the conditional statement is false; every other case gives a true result.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			Again, this result might seem very abstract. 
			Here is a real-life situation that could be modeled by <m>(m \land\neg\,p)\rightarrow r</m>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Suppose <m>m</m> is <q>We order meatballs,</q> <m>p</m> is <q>We order pasta,</q> and <m>r</m> is <q>Ruba is happy.</q> The statement <m>(m \land\neg\,p)\rightarrow r</m> is then <q>If we order meatballs and don't order pasta, then Ruba is happy.</q> If <m>m</m> is true (we order meatballs), <m>p</m> is false (we don't order pasta), and <m>r</m> is false (Ruba is not happy), then the statement is false, because the hypothesis is true but the conclusion is false. In all other cases, the statement is true.
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<exercises>
		<!-- Sources: Exercise 1 is adapted from Benjamin Jackson's MATH 105
		Objective 6 warm-up and slides, Exercises 2 and 9 from his Objective 6
		slides, and Exercise 3a from an Objective 6 clicker question (Walla
		Walla University, Fall 2026). Exercises 7, 8, 10, and 11 are based on
		his MATH 105 written-homework questions 05b, 05c, 05a, and 06c.
		Exercises 3b, 4, 5, 6, and 12 are new.
		-->
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise><!-- #1 (Obj 6 warm-up and slides: Mia's promise) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Mia tells Noah, <q>If you help me move on Saturday, then I'll buy you pizza.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								List all four possibilities for what could happen on Saturday (Noah helps or doesn't; Mia buys pizza or doesn't). For each one, decide whether Mia kept her promise or broke it.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								In which situation can Noah honestly say that Mia lied?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>h</m> be <q>Noah helps Mia move</q> and <m>z</m> be <q>Mia buys Noah pizza.</q> Explain how your answers match the truth table for <m>h \to z</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>Noah helps?</cell>
									<cell>Mia buys pizza?</cell>
									<cell>Promise</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>Yes</cell>
									<cell>Yes</cell>
									<cell>Kept</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>Yes</cell>
									<cell>No</cell>
									<cell>Broken</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>No</cell>
									<cell>Yes</cell>
									<cell>Kept</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>No</cell>
									<cell>No</cell>
									<cell>Kept</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								In the third row Mia was simply generous, and in the fourth row Noah didn't help, so Mia never owed him anything.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Only when Noah helps and Mia does not buy pizza.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The conditional <m>h \to z</m> is false only when <m>h</m> is true and <m>z</m> is false, which is exactly the one situation in which Mia broke her promise. In the other three rows, <m>h \to z</m> is true, just as Mia kept her promise.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #2 (Obj 6 slides: shelf) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>p</m> be <q>To put this shelf together, I need a screwdriver,</q> and let <m>q</m> be <q>To put this shelf together, I need a wrench.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Make one truth table with columns for <m>p \land q</m>, <m>p \lor q</m>, and <m>\neg q</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Suppose it turns out that you need a wrench but not a screwdriver. Use your table to decide which of the three statements are true.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \land q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								This situation is row 3 (<m>p</m> false, <m>q</m> true). In that row, <m>p \land q</m> is false, <m>p \lor q</m> is true, and <m>\neg q</m> is false. So only <q>I need a screwdriver or a wrench</q> is true.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #3 (part a from an Obj 6 clicker question; part b new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					The rows of each truth table below are in an unusual order, and one value is missing. Fill in the missing value, and explain how you know.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \oplus q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell><fillin characters="3"/></cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell><fillin characters="3"/></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								F. The exclusive or <m>p \oplus q</m> is true when exactly one of <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> is true. When both are true, it is false.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								T. A conditional is false only when its antecedent is true and its consequent is false. Here <m>p</m> is false, so <m>p \to q</m> is true.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #4 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many rows does a truth table need for a statement with one proposition? With two? With three? With four?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Explain the pattern. How many rows would a statement with five propositions need?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								One proposition needs 2 rows, two need 4, three need 8, and four need 16.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Each new proposition can be true or false in every row of the previous table, so adding a proposition doubles the number of rows. A statement with <m>n</m> propositions needs <m>2^n</m> rows, so five propositions need <m>2^5 = 32</m> rows.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #5 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Create a truth table for each statement. Then describe in words when the statement is true.
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\neg p \land q</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\neg(p \lor q)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>p \to \neg q</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(p \lor q) \to \neg p</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg p \land q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is true only in row 3, when <m>p</m> is false and <m>q</m> is true.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg(p \lor q)</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is true only in row 4, when <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are both false.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \to \neg q</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is false only in row 1, when <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are both true.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>(p \lor q) \to \neg p</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is false in rows 1 and 2, the rows in which <m>p</m> is true.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #6 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose <m>p</m> is true, <m>q</m> is false, and <m>r</m> is true. Find the truth value of each statement without making a full truth table. (Work from the inside of the parentheses out.)
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>p \land (q \lor r)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\neg p \lor (q \to r)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(p \lor q) \to \neg r</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\neg(p \land q) \land \neg r</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>q \lor r</m> is <m>F \lor T = T</m>, so <m>p \land (q \lor r)</m> is <m>T \land T</m>, which is <alert>true</alert>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\neg p</m> is false, and <m>q \to r</m> is <m>F \to T</m>, which is true. So the statement is <m>F \lor T</m>, which is <alert>true</alert>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>p \lor q</m> is true and <m>\neg r</m> is false, so the statement is <m>T \to F</m>, which is <alert>false</alert>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>p \land q</m> is false, so <m>\neg(p \land q)</m> is true. But <m>\neg r</m> is false, so the statement is <m>T \land F</m>, which is <alert>false</alert>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #7 (Written Homework question 05b) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>F</m> represent <q>She finishes her bachelor's degree</q> and <m>G</m> represent <q>She gets the job.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write <q>She finishes her bachelor's degree but doesn't get the job</q> in symbols.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Write <q>If she finishes her bachelor's degree, then she gets the job</q> in symbols.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Make one truth table with columns for both statements. What do you notice about the two columns?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>F \land \neg G</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>F \to G</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>F</m></cell>
									<cell><m>G</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\neg G</m></cell>
									<cell><m>F \land \neg G</m></cell>
									<cell><m>F \to G</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The two columns are opposites in every row: whenever one statement is true, the other is false. This makes sense, since <q>she finishes her degree but doesn't get the job</q> is exactly the situation that would make the conditional false. (We will return to this idea in <xref ref="sec-logic-equivalence"/>.)
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #8 (Written Homework question 05c) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the following propositions:
				</p>
				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Let <m>P</m> be <q>The restaurant is open.</q></p></li>
						<li><p>Let <m>Q</m> be <q>The chef is working tonight.</q></p></li>
						<li><p>Let <m>R</m> be <q>We can make a reservation.</q></p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Translate <m>P \land \neg Q</m> into smooth English.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Translate <m>(P \land Q) \to R</m> into smooth English.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Create a truth table for <m>(P \land Q) \to R</m>. In which situation is the statement false? Describe it in words.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<q>The restaurant is open, but the chef is not working tonight.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<q>If the restaurant is open and the chef is working tonight, then we can make a reservation.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>P</m></cell>
									<cell><m>Q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>R</m></cell>
									<cell><m>P \land Q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>(P \land Q) \to R</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>5</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>6</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>7</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>8</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is false only in row 2: the restaurant is open and the chef is working tonight, but we still can't make a reservation.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #9 (Obj 6 slides: Service Day) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the statement <q>If it is raining and there is lightning, then Service Day will be canceled.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Choose letters for the three propositions, and write the statement in symbols.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Create a truth table for the statement.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Suppose it rains all day but there is no lightning, and Service Day goes ahead as planned. Was the statement true or false? Which row of your table describes this situation?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>r</m> be <q>It is raining,</q> <m>l</m> be <q>There is lightning,</q> and <m>c</m> be <q>Service Day will be canceled.</q> The statement is <m>(r \land l) \to c</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>r</m></cell>
									<cell><m>l</m></cell>
									<cell><m>c</m></cell>
									<cell><m>r \land l</m></cell>
									<cell><m>(r \land l) \to c</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>5</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>6</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>7</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>8</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is false only in row 2, when it rains and there is lightning but Service Day is not canceled.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								True. This is row 4 (<m>r</m> true, <m>l</m> false, <m>c</m> false). Since there was no lightning, the antecedent <m>r \land l</m> is false, so the conditional is true no matter what happened to Service Day.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #10 (Written Homework question 05a) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the following propositions:
				</p>
				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Let <m>A</m> be <q>Artificial intelligence will surpass human intelligence.</q></p></li>
						<li><p>Let <m>J</m> be <q>Many jobs will be automated.</q></p></li>
						<li><p>Let <m>E</m> be <q>Education systems need to be reformed.</q></p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write <q>If artificial intelligence surpasses human intelligence, then many jobs will be automated and education systems need to be reformed</q> in symbols.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Write <q>Many jobs will be automated, but education systems don't need to be reformed</q> in symbols.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Create a truth table for your answer to (a). In which rows is the statement false?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \to (J \land E)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>J \land \neg E</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>A</m></cell>
									<cell><m>J</m></cell>
									<cell><m>E</m></cell>
									<cell><m>J \land E</m></cell>
									<cell><m>A \to (J \land E)</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>5</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>6</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>7</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>8</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								The statement is false in rows 2, 3, and 4: artificial intelligence surpasses human intelligence, but it is not the case that many jobs are automated <em>and</em> education systems need reform.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #11 (Written Homework question 06c) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Create a truth table for the statement <m>[(p \to q) \land (q \to r)] \to (p \to r)</m>. What do you notice about the final column?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>row</cell>
						<cell><m>p</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>r</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \to q</m></cell>
						<cell><m>q \to r</m></cell>
						<cell><m>p \to r</m></cell>
						<cell><m>(p \to q) \land (q \to r)</m></cell>
						<cell><m>[(p \to q) \land (q \to r)] \to (p \to r)</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>1</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>2</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>3</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>4</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>5</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>6</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>7</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>8</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>F</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
						<cell>T</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					The final column is true in every row, so the statement is true no matter what the truth values of <m>p</m>, <m>q</m>, and <m>r</m> are. In words, if <m>p</m> leads to <m>q</m> and <m>q</m> leads to <m>r</m>, then <m>p</m> leads to <m>r</m>. This fact is called the <em>transitive property</em>, and it appears again in <xref ref="sec-logic-arguments"/>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise><!-- #12 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A car rental company has this rule: <q>You can rent a car if you are at least 25 years old, or if you are at least 21 years old and you pay a young-driver fee.</q> Let <m>p</m> be <q>You are at least 25,</q> <m>q</m> be <q>You are at least 21,</q> and <m>r</m> be <q>You pay the young-driver fee.</q>
				</p>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Write the condition for renting a car in symbols.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Create a truth table for your answer to (a). In which rows can you rent a car?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Two rows of the table describe situations that cannot actually happen. Which rows are they, and why?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>p \lor (q \land r)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>row</cell>
									<cell><m>p</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q</m></cell>
									<cell><m>r</m></cell>
									<cell><m>q \land r</m></cell>
									<cell><m>p \lor (q \land r)</m></cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>1</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>2</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>5</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>6</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>7</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>T</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>8</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
									<cell>F</cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
							<p>
								You can rent a car in rows 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5: whenever you are at least 25, or when you are at least 21 and pay the fee.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Rows 3 and 4, where <m>p</m> is true and <m>q</m> is false. Anyone who is at least 25 is also at least 21, so these rows can't happen. A truth table lists every combination of truth values, even ones that real-world facts rule out.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

	</exercises>
</section>
